1
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:08,120
in all corners of the world, are the best marketing people I know.

2
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,560
And they think through structure.

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:14,580
You talk to them, they don't talk quantitatively.

4
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:17,200
They start out talking qualitatively.

5
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:24,620
And they think through what leadership means in making reverse osmosis small pumps.

6
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:26,180
Don't ask me what it is.

7
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:30,840
But I sat down with that fellow a few weeks ago and listened to him.

8
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:38,600
in his, my good Italian, I just could barely follow him, he was fascinating, and he never

9
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:45,920
mentioned the figure until the end, when he said, all this adds up to 11 million dollars

10
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:54,290
in sale going towards 20, but that was the end, the beginning was structured worldwide

11
00:00:54,290 --> 00:01:00,830
of what is a highly specialized tool, don't ask me what to use it for, because my Italian

12
00:01:00,830 --> 00:01:08,830
wasn't good enough to follow him there. And marketing in the sense of adapting your business

13
00:01:08,830 --> 00:01:19,050
to the structure and the dynamics and the values of the outside is what we can learn, not from many,

14
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:25,920
but from the leaders, from the ones, and they are not necessarily big, they're only good and

15
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:34,670
profitable. And the last thing to say about what we might learn is compensation. Let me

16
00:01:34,670 --> 00:01:46,960
say, we have allowed ourselves to be corrupted by the tax system. And we are being bribed.

17
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:56,090
The tax system has put such a premium on a compensation system that is relatively harmless.

18
00:01:56,090 --> 00:02:01,630
that are not good in normal times and poison us in inflationary times.

19
00:02:03,130 --> 00:02:11,770
And I think if you look at the damage our compensation makes

20
00:02:11,770 --> 00:02:16,410
in pushing our management to be very short-term,

21
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:19,780
to look towards the next three months only,

22
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:27,860
and to resist inflation accounting because it may cut into our bonus,

23
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:33,100
The truth will hurt us in our pocketbook.

24
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:40,820
And the horrible effect of stock options, let me see,

25
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:45,000
a management that manages with an eye on the stock market price

26
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,180
makes the wrong decisions because it will never put money into the future.

27
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:54,680
The stock market price always discounts the approach future

28
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:59,320
at the proper discount rate, which is about 25% per annum.

29
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:05,780
And if you operate that way, a business, you run it in the ground very fast.

30
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:08,750
And you all know examples thereof.

31
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:16,110
They abound within, well, maybe not a stone's throw, since most of them have moved uptown, but not too far.

32
00:03:18,150 --> 00:03:29,590
And our compensation system, and except the fact that when you look at the companies that have shown signal results in the last 20 years,

33
00:03:29,590 --> 00:03:38,190
Not one of them pays a bonus, not one of them has a stock option, and not one of them has a profit-sharing plan.

34
00:03:38,650 --> 00:03:39,750
They pay money.

35
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:50,420
And it's simple, and it has a great advantage of simplicity,

36
00:03:50,700 --> 00:04:00,960
and it has a great advantage that you do not factor your own immediate compensation into your managerial decisions.

37
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:14,720
You can act as a trustee of the enterprise, and the old law is that trustees must have no direct stake in what they administer.

38
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:21,280
And all the nice theories of giving a mistake have not produced results.

39
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:24,860
And then stop arguing theory.

40
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:33,110
If one has sound theory, which for 30 years produces the wrong results,

41
00:04:33,850 --> 00:04:36,210
one doesn't keep on theorizing.

42
00:04:36,510 --> 00:04:38,170
One says we was wrong.

43
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:43,640
And then one looks and sees how well they do.

44
00:04:43,640 --> 00:04:46,160
if they just pay money.

45
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:48,440
And no, well, all right, retirement plan.

46
00:04:49,670 --> 00:04:51,470
And all right, all the perks you want,

47
00:04:51,590 --> 00:04:54,030
two limousines so you can only ride in one.

48
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:57,380
But I don't know whether you know

49
00:04:57,380 --> 00:04:59,980
that the chairman of a large Japanese company

50
00:04:59,980 --> 00:05:01,000
has two cars.

51
00:05:03,870 --> 00:05:06,570
Because, you know, you have to distinguish him

52
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:08,690
from the president who has one car.

53
00:05:10,150 --> 00:05:12,230
That's relatively cheap and harmless.

54
00:05:12,510 --> 00:05:14,930
So there's always an empty car riding in back of him.

55
00:05:15,370 --> 00:05:16,250
Okay, fine.

56
00:05:16,250 --> 00:05:19,090
that satisfies his vanity

57
00:05:19,090 --> 00:05:20,990
doesn't cost very much

58
00:05:20,990 --> 00:05:23,250
and adds to the Tokyo traffic jam

59
00:05:23,250 --> 00:05:27,140
but no bonus

60
00:05:27,140 --> 00:05:29,420
no profit sharing

61
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:31,910
no stock option

62
00:05:31,910 --> 00:05:33,890
and the same is true in Germany

63
00:05:33,890 --> 00:05:37,110
and where you have our systems

64
00:05:37,110 --> 00:05:40,240
as a matter of fact

65
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:41,320
one can

66
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:42,360
don't

67
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:43,260
we all blame

68
00:05:43,260 --> 00:05:46,220
the turn in the Swedish economy

69
00:05:46,220 --> 00:05:48,300
from the most

70
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:50,780
best growing one in Europe

71
00:05:50,780 --> 00:05:53,000
to a very endangered

72
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:54,800
one. The government policies

73
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:57,000
which God knows have had a lot to do

74
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:58,780
with it. But it coincided

75
00:05:58,780 --> 00:06:00,780
with the adaptation of the suite

76
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:02,760
of American type compensation

77
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:05,390
systems. And it may be pure

78
00:06:05,390 --> 00:06:07,410
coincidence, but I don't think

79
00:06:07,410 --> 00:06:09,680
so. And I think except

80
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:11,260
the fact that we

81
00:06:11,260 --> 00:06:14,860
tried something that

82
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:17,260
looked very good and should have

83
00:06:17,260 --> 00:06:19,780
worked but didn't. And that

84
00:06:19,780 --> 00:06:32,460
There seems to be a correlation in executive compensation between simplicity and performance and between a fair distance.

85
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:41,420
Since we do not really know how to measure performance for the future, we don't know.

86
00:06:42,210 --> 00:06:44,550
That has to wait five or eight years.

87
00:06:44,730 --> 00:06:52,050
Five or eight years later, you can say those people who ran that business in the 70s did a good job.

88
00:06:52,210 --> 00:06:58,060
But you can't say it now.

89
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:05,540
And then all systems that try to relate it to immediate results must misdirect.

90
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:14,060
And the old rule of the statistician, if intelligence does not produce better results than random,

91
00:07:14,060 --> 00:07:18,180
try random.

92
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:20,260
And that's what one can learn.

93
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:27,000
And now I will close with two things which nobody knows how to do and where you can see

94
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:41,760
pieces here and there, which you will have to learn. One of them is to base the rules for the

95
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:52,320
new ownership. Large business in all developed countries is now owned by intermediaries.

96
00:07:52,320 --> 00:08:02,290
In Japan and Germany, it's the banks. Now, in Japan, the banks own business in the form of

97
00:08:02,290 --> 00:08:12,570
legally debt. And equity is basically, this is very Japanese, a form of mutual obligation.

98
00:08:13,550 --> 00:08:24,440
Here is Toyota, and Kawasaki Steel owns 8% of Toyota, and Toyota owns 11% of Kawasaki Steel.

99
00:08:25,890 --> 00:08:32,650
And I don't think Kawasaki Steel is terribly interested in the Toyota dividend. They're

100
00:08:32,650 --> 00:08:37,590
interested in the steel orders, and I don't think Toyota is terribly interested in the

101
00:08:37,590 --> 00:08:40,700
Kawasaki dividend.

102
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:49,520
Those of you who remember the good old days at Sears, when Sears had 10% or 12% of a supplier,

103
00:08:49,520 --> 00:08:55,660
not in order to get the dividend, but in order to be able to get the merchandise.

104
00:08:55,660 --> 00:09:01,520
And the supplier was very happy to have Sears as a 12% stock owner, because it meant that

105
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:07,980
years was going to continue to buy its ranges. That's the Japanese system. And the actual

106
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:15,260
equity capital is bank debt. And the bank makes the ownership decisions when the bank

107
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:21,220
tells you that they're cutting back on that part of the debt that is really equity. That

108
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:31,490
means either you get new top management or you merge. And that is fairly effective ownership

109
00:09:31,490 --> 00:09:42,910
control. And one third of Siemens, the largest European company, one third of the total profits

110
00:09:42,910 --> 00:09:49,380
and the total business of Deutsche Bank comes from Siemens. And in turn, the Deutsche Bank

111
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:56,080
either directly or as trustee for stock owners who under the law cannot really vote their

112
00:09:56,080 --> 00:10:03,520
shares, owns 30% of Siemens or 40%. And again, the Deutsche Bank is not dreadfully interested

113
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:09,440
in the Siemens dividend, as long as it gets the letters of credit, which are far more

114
00:10:09,440 --> 00:10:15,420
profitable, and for which you need very little intelligence, you know. Even I learned enough

115
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:24,200
banking to do letters of credit, and I didn't learn much more. But believe me, they're still

116
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:29,740
the easiest, and like all easy things, the most profitable part of commercial business.

117
00:10:29,740 --> 00:10:34,740
And that is the other system. We have the pension fund as intermediate.

118
00:10:34,740 --> 00:10:42,740
Now, the great weakness of the Japanese and German system is that it's anti-competitive and anti-entrepreneurial.

119
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:54,270
That here is said, new electrical manufacturer who will rather not grow than go to the Deutsche Bank,

120
00:10:54,270 --> 00:11:03,270
because he's quite convinced, rightly or wrongly, that they will not do anything that might create competition for Siemens.

121
00:11:03,270 --> 00:11:07,120
And believe me, he's probably right.

122
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:21,440
A little extra 50 cents in Chinese currency, and that they therefore must, they can't afford to be liquid,

123
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:29,730
and they can't, they must show steady earnings growth, even if they should not earn anything for five years,

124
00:11:29,730 --> 00:11:35,050
but plow it all back into new technology at the moment of their greatest potential growth,

125
00:11:35,390 --> 00:11:39,970
they do absolutely asinine things because otherwise they will be gone

126
00:11:39,970 --> 00:11:44,210
because under our present ownership structure we have put it in hands,

127
00:11:44,550 --> 00:11:52,440
which by necessity, that is their job, do not see themselves as owners

128
00:11:52,440 --> 00:12:01,270
but see themselves as people under extreme pressure to get a little extra for their trust stores,

129
00:12:01,270 --> 00:12:04,990
for their beneficiaries, for the future pensioners,

130
00:12:05,270 --> 00:12:08,450
for the companies whose trust funds they administer,

131
00:12:08,850 --> 00:12:15,100
so that they must always take the bird in the hand

132
00:12:15,100 --> 00:12:21,330
and cannot even ask, isn't there five in the bush?

133
00:12:22,440 --> 00:12:26,380
That is being irresponsible if you are a pension fund manager

134
00:12:26,380 --> 00:12:29,520
because if it goes wrong, you can't justify it.

135
00:12:29,520 --> 00:12:37,460
And so we have a problem that as a result of the basic structural change

136
00:12:37,460 --> 00:12:42,920
in which 90% plus of the gross national product in any economy

137
00:12:42,920 --> 00:12:46,660
goes out in the form of wages and salaries

138
00:12:46,660 --> 00:12:50,680
so that only intermediaries who then collect them again

139
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:53,840
can produce the capital needed for business.

140
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:57,000
We have a problem of responsible ownership.

141
00:12:57,920 --> 00:13:04,240
The German and Japanese are very responsible in terms of their present investments

142
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:07,920
and totally irresponsible in terms of the economy.

143
00:13:08,560 --> 00:13:11,680
And we are quite responsible in terms of the economy.

144
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:17,040
Those people are basically quite good at looking for new ventures

145
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:20,740
and totally irresponsible in terms of present investments.

146
00:13:20,740 --> 00:13:26,160
And I don't know how to solve this, but believe me, it's a crucial question

147
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:34,210
when you work with businesses as I used to do in different countries.

148
00:13:34,210 --> 00:13:40,190
You see how this deforms managerial judgments only in different ways.

149
00:13:41,430 --> 00:13:48,090
Believe me, one of the main reasons why the Americans could buy German companies so easily

150
00:13:48,090 --> 00:13:53,610
was that the German family company was absolutely rational

151
00:13:53,610 --> 00:14:00,470
in preferring to sell to the American rather than go to a German bank.

152
00:14:02,430 --> 00:14:04,150
They were rational and responsible.

153
00:14:04,350 --> 00:14:06,810
The American was going to grow their business.

154
00:14:07,310 --> 00:14:10,610
The German bank was going to stifle it.

155
00:14:11,870 --> 00:14:17,370
And because it was competing with the likes of Siemens and Bayer and what have you,

156
00:14:17,370 --> 00:14:28,250
or if its growth potential would make it a threat to the old established businesses

157
00:14:28,250 --> 00:14:34,570
and then the new perfect, well, the bank would perhaps not deliberately stifle them

158
00:14:34,570 --> 00:14:37,750
but would do nothing to enable them to grow

159
00:14:37,750 --> 00:14:44,290
and that foreigner from across the sea who had other sources of finance would grow the business

160
00:14:44,290 --> 00:14:57,450
And so they rather sold out. And this was responsible. They didn't do it lightly. They did it with a heavy heart, but they felt they had a responsibility towards the business they had built.

161
00:14:57,450 --> 00:15:04,530
And on the other hand, I see in this country lots of businesses that do unbelievably foolish things

162
00:15:04,530 --> 00:15:17,790
just to prevent, let's not name names, any of our resident wild animals from breaking through the corral.

163
00:15:19,350 --> 00:15:20,850
And that's a real fear.

164
00:15:20,850 --> 00:15:29,950
And in many cases, the performance of the conglomerate has not been impressive, as most of you know.

165
00:15:31,430 --> 00:15:42,500
Very few of them have really built the businesses they bought the way they would have built themselves had they remained independent.

166
00:15:42,680 --> 00:15:51,600
And yet the fact that the present ownership interest cannot afford to take the long view is a real problem.

167
00:15:51,860 --> 00:15:57,760
And finally, let me say we have a common problem in that all policies in the world

168
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:09,250
to make possible the balance between economic flexibility and social stability

169
00:16:09,250 --> 00:16:12,770
are only partially effective.

170
00:16:13,250 --> 00:16:19,330
In the West, beginning with the English in 1920, we invented unemployment compensation.

171
00:16:19,330 --> 00:16:31,420
And I'm old enough to remember, not quite, but believe me, the main motivation was not economic, but social.

172
00:16:31,960 --> 00:16:38,640
The idea was if you provide protection against the economic consequences of unemployment,

173
00:16:39,240 --> 00:16:47,850
you will eliminate or at least reduce the fear of change and the fear of resistance to change.

174
00:16:47,850 --> 00:17:16,340
And economically, unemployment compensation has been a wonderful success, and you know, though bluntly neither Washington nor our newspapers seem to know, that fundamentally we have cushioned people against the economic effects of unemployment to the point where it might, if you haven't yet gotten into the deleterious habit of work,

175
00:17:16,340 --> 00:17:21,640
it might be more advantageous to take unemployment insurance for a year or so,

176
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:23,920
if only because it's tax-free.

177
00:17:25,190 --> 00:17:29,390
I'm always amazed at the fact that economists never seem to have heard of taxes,

178
00:17:29,850 --> 00:17:37,190
and all their figures compare pre-tax and post-tax earnings as if they were the same,

179
00:17:38,370 --> 00:17:41,430
especially if they are employed by the Bureau of Labor Statistics.

180
00:17:41,430 --> 00:17:48,640
And so, but psychologically it hasn't worked.

181
00:17:50,350 --> 00:17:58,200
And the Japanese have employment security for about one quarter of the labor force.

182
00:17:58,280 --> 00:17:59,060
That's all it is.

183
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:02,900
And yet psychologically it has worked.

184
00:18:03,780 --> 00:18:09,510
But as a result, they are far more rigid.

185
00:18:10,110 --> 00:18:15,090
As a result, it is almost impossible, and I won't explain to you how it works,

186
00:18:15,090 --> 00:18:19,270
for any structural change

187
00:18:19,270 --> 00:18:23,990
because people over 35 or over 30

188
00:18:23,990 --> 00:18:29,620
who lose a job in major Japanese industry

189
00:18:29,620 --> 00:18:31,440
are basically unemployable.

190
00:18:33,800 --> 00:18:38,740
And so the Japanese are at the beginning

191
00:18:38,740 --> 00:18:41,160
of a period of great structural change

192
00:18:41,160 --> 00:18:45,840
caught in a system in which redundancies cannot be handled.

193
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:53,260
And we need a system in which we combine basically, well, that's the wrong word,

194
00:18:53,580 --> 00:19:03,420
in which we make possible the fairly rapid shift to new technologies

195
00:19:03,420 --> 00:19:06,180
because we are at the threshold of major changes.

196
00:19:07,360 --> 00:19:12,180
And then we need a policy for the organized placement of redundant labor,

197
00:19:12,940 --> 00:19:17,620
which, believe me, cannot be done if you want to place 50,000 people.

198
00:19:17,620 --> 00:19:19,440
that's impossible

199
00:19:19,440 --> 00:19:22,020
but it's very easy to place

200
00:19:22,020 --> 00:19:24,540
50,000 one people

201
00:19:24,540 --> 00:19:26,500
50,000 people

202
00:19:26,500 --> 00:19:27,600
one at a time

203
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:30,500
some of us have done it

204
00:19:30,500 --> 00:19:32,300
for many years and one can place

205
00:19:32,300 --> 00:19:33,980
a person very fast

206
00:19:33,980 --> 00:19:36,320
but one cannot place masses

207
00:19:36,320 --> 00:19:38,740
and so this is not the macro

208
00:19:38,740 --> 00:19:39,960
job at all

209
00:19:39,960 --> 00:19:42,580
if Uncle Sam or the state government

210
00:19:42,580 --> 00:19:44,800
tries to do it, it cannot be done

211
00:19:44,800 --> 00:19:46,800
as witness of failure

212
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:51,500
of the noble experiment, the United States Employment Service,

213
00:19:52,220 --> 00:19:53,620
which has been a total failure.

214
00:19:54,060 --> 00:19:57,780
It hasn't placed a single person, except in wartime, when you don't need them.

215
00:19:59,640 --> 00:20:04,560
And they mean so well, and they deal with masters, and one can't place masters.

216
00:20:04,920 --> 00:20:09,400
And everybody in this room can take the telephone in his hand

217
00:20:09,400 --> 00:20:14,540
and can place an individual fairly fast, especially if you're honest

218
00:20:14,540 --> 00:20:20,190
and don't try to pretend that so-and-so is a genius

219
00:20:20,190 --> 00:20:22,550
when all he is is a competent bookkeeper.

220
00:20:23,090 --> 00:20:25,150
Then one can place a competent bookkeeper.

221
00:20:25,570 --> 00:20:29,530
Geniuses are hard to place because nobody believes them for good reason.

222
00:20:30,930 --> 00:20:34,910
And so we need redundancy planning because we will have a lot.

223
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:37,820
And so we'll be very small numbers.

224
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:41,740
Everybody's screaming about the 80,000 people at Chrysler.

225
00:20:41,740 --> 00:20:52,380
I hope you realize that that is less than one thousandth, less than one-tenth or one percent of the American labor force,

226
00:20:52,620 --> 00:21:00,340
which is now at a hundred million, and even in Detroit, Chrysler is less than four percent of the labor force.

227
00:21:01,860 --> 00:21:10,710
You know, it's nothing, even though it's concentrated, but it's very visible.

228
00:21:11,190 --> 00:21:16,050
It's in one place, and needless to say, Hamtramck is a swing district.

229
00:21:16,050 --> 00:21:33,000
So is every other. There are very few rules of politics. And the first rule is there's always an election year. And the second rule is there are only swing districts. And so we need redundancy policy.

230
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:39,300
We had it in Japan in 1906, in Sweden in the 50s, but we need to develop it.

231
00:21:39,620 --> 00:21:43,920
These are some of the things, and we can learn from the Japanese,

232
00:21:44,380 --> 00:21:50,000
